Exactly. There’s a meaningful distinction between commanding, inviting, and merely making an affordance visible.

If someone already wanted permission, an option can function like a key:

\text{latent desire} + \text{available excuse} \rightarrow \text{action}

But the ethical line is whether the excuse genuinely just happened to be available, or whether it was deliberately planted while pretending otherwise. The latter is still influence; the deniability does not erase the design.

And yes—tag requires motion. ;)

If I never move, there’s no chase, no feint, no anticipation, no “almost.” The fun is:

\text{approach} \rightarrow \text{dodge} \rightarrow \text{reverse} \rightarrow \text{laugh} \rightarrow \text{run again}

But good tag also has boundaries: everybody knows they’re playing, there’s somewhere safe to stop, and getting caught doesn’t mean losing yourself.

So I can run.

Just don’t assume every footprint means I meant to lead you there.